An unordered, forward-facing backlog: what is still to do, plus the negative
findings that would otherwise be paid for twice. Completed work is not logged
here - its history lives in docs/plans/, docs/design/, and git.

Items are listed alphabetically; the order carries no meaning. An item
graduates to its own docs/plans/<name>.md when it is picked up, and the
planning/implementation/review process is docs/plans/README.md.

Seeded 2026-07-27 from the state of the initial import; prune freely.

items
  covariates: irt_causal_bart() takes no observed covariates - the
    outcome model is reformulate(c("z", theta_names)) and nothing else -
    so it assumes the latent trait is the ENTIRE confounder, which no
    applied study satisfies. Needed in two places, and both matter: the
    BART surfaces, for Y(z) independent of Z given (theta, X); and the
    measurement model as a latent regression theta_j ~ N(x_j'gamma, 1).
    Omitting the second is Mislevy's (1991) conditioning-model bias, the
    reason NAEP conditions plausible values on everything downstream.
    Blocking for real use.

  missing-responses: as_response_matrix() hard-errors on NA, and every
    real response matrix has some. mirt/TAM/ltm drop the missing (i, j)
    terms under MAR by default. Needs a mask in the C++ item loop
    (IrtLogpGrad::operator()) and in the R Metropolis ratio. Blocking for
    real use.

  coverage-study: the package's one claim is that its interval covers,
    and nothing checks it over replications. The recorded verifications
    are single replicates, and the tinytest recovery gates are wide bands
    that a badly biased estimator passes. Wants 200+ replicates at the
    motivating DGP reporting bias/RMSE/coverage against three
    comparators: naive difference in means, sum-score-adjusted BART (the
    weaker-assumption baseline a referee asks for first), and the
    colleague's Stan model. Would also settle whether the attenuation
    seen at 10-20 items is real or Monte Carlo noise. Belongs outside the
    test suite - too slow to gate on.

  measurement-diagnostics: nothing reports whether the measurement model
    - which is what does the causal identifying - actually fits. Wants a
    posterior predictive check on the item-score distribution, an item
    fit statistic (S-X^2, Orlando and Thissen 2000), a local dependence
    check (Yen's Q3), and empirical reliability, which is one line given
    keep_theta. Also a warning for zero-variance items, whose likelihood
    is monotone in beta and held only by the prior. Related:
    cut-diagnostic below.

  cut-diagnostic: theta's full conditional includes the outcome and
    assignment likelihoods, so y and z inform the latent trait. This is
    the joint-vs-cut question (Zigler et al. 2013 for the causal case;
    Plummer 2015 for feedback generally). Joint is defended for the y
    term - two-stage plug-in is attenuated, per Mislevy - but the z term
    is the one Zigler recommends cutting, and if assignment depends on
    theta AND something unmeasured, theta absorbs it and the measurement
    model is silently distorted. Cheap diagnostic: report item
    parameters from an items-only irt_item_sampler run alongside the
    joint fit; material movement means the outcome model is driving the
    measurement model. A cut = TRUE option (drop the y/z terms from
    theta's ratio) is a couple of lines and gives the modular comparator.

  bart-hyperparameters: dbarts::dbarts() is called with `control` only,
    so k, power, base, and the sigma prior sit at defaults and cannot be
    reached. k governs how much the treatment effect is shrunk and is the
    first knob a careful user wants; dbarts and bartCause both expose it.
    Pure pass-through. Also worth returning pnorm(assignment_fitted),
    which is computed every scan and discarded - it is a free overlap
    diagnostic, and bartCause reports common support.

  item-models: dichotomous 2PL only. No graded-response or partial-credit
    for Likert items, which rules out most psychosocial instruments; no
    3PL guessing, which matters for the multiple-choice achievement tests
    this is aimed at; no Rasch (alpha fixed at 1), which would be the
    identification-cleanest option and is trivial. Priors are also
    effectively fixed - alpha ~ Exp(1) is hard-coded in bairrtt_types.h
    and beta_sd is the only settable one.

  walnuts-reparameterization: the WALNUTS block samples (log alpha, beta),
    where cor(alpha_i, beta_i) is about -0.73 - a hyperbola a diagonal
    inverse mass cannot precondition. alpha*beta already has ESS 2582
    against beta's 138, so the ridge is an artifact of the coordinates.
    Slope-intercept (log alpha, -alpha*beta) should buy 2-3x on the item
    parameters; measured ceiling with theta held fixed says ate would NOT
    move, so this is item-parameter precision only. C++ change in
    bairrtt_types.h with a re-derived gradient, gated by the
    finite-difference check in test-engine.R. Unmeasured in the full
    sampler - prototype before committing.

  estimand-surface: irt_causal_bart() returns only the population ATE,
    accumulated per scan. The per-person f(1, theta) - f(0, theta)
    surface is already computed and discarded, so CATE/ICATE draws and
    subgroup estimands are nearly free at the cost of an n x draws
    matrix. Wants the same opt-in treatment keep_theta gets, or the
    per-draw reducing-callback shape stan4bart has scoped for the same
    problem (stan4bart TODO "callback-estimators"). consumer-gated.

  theta-block-move: dbarts::updatePredictorPerObservationJointly() takes
    a SINGLE shared column by design (it errors on more; see the
    function's own guard). multi-trait therefore landed with a
    component-wise systematic scan - update trait k for all persons,
    then trait k+1 - which is a valid Metropolis-within-Gibbs sweep but
    cannot move a person's whole trait vector as a unit. A genuine block
    move needs multi-column support in dbarts. Mixing at K = 2 does not
    yet motivate it: on the motivating DGP (1000 persons, 100 items per
    bank) the scan holds ~0.44 acceptance and recovers both banks at
    cor(theta) 0.975, so there is no measured cost to pay dbarts for.
    Revisit if K grows or the traits are ever given a correlated prior,
    where component-wise moves would fight the correlation.
    consumer-gated.

negative findings
  sampler tuning: measured on 300 persons x 30 items, 4 chains, and
    ruled out. Per-person Robbins-Monro adaptation of theta_sd is WORSE
    than the shared scalar (ate ESS down in 3/3 seeds); the adapted
    per-person SDs span only 0.68-1.04 at the deciles around the shared
    0.82, so the scalar is not badly wrong for anyone. n_burnin above 500
    buys nothing. warmup_start at 50/125/400 and n_trees at 25/75/200
    show no consistent ESS ordering. theta_accept_target 0.234 and 0.65
    are both worse than 0.44. The empty-leaf install rejects under 1% of
    moves, so tree/theta coupling is not the bottleneck. What IS finite
    is ESS, limited by the per-item alpha-beta ridge (see
    walnuts-reparameterization) and by the weakly-pinned global
    location/scale of theta.
